 In cross-table transfer, prior studies commonly assess semantic knowledge by comparing the intended knowledge with altered alternatives, such as corrupted or shuffled semantics, or with omission-based controls \citep{dinh2022lift,hegselmann2023tabllm}.
 Corrupted or shuffled alternatives may introduce misleading semantic information, degrading prediction and making the intended knowledge appear beneficial partly because the alternative performs worse.
 Omission-based controls can also be difficult to interpret because removing semantic knowledge can simultaneously remove other information available to the model, change how the remaining information is represented, or alter the transfer procedure \citep{hewitt2019designing,elazar2021amnesic,sturmfels2020visualizing}.
-Consequently, a large semantic-ablation effect does not by itself establish a comparable improvement in predictiion.
+Consequently, a large semantic-ablation effect does not by itself establish a comparable improvement in prediction.
 
 % P3. 
 We therefore separate two evaluation questions that are often not clearly distinguished in semantic ablations.
